 "widgets": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 64,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "Exfiltrate your weights",
   "size": "xl",
   "weight": "bold"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 128,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "675 pts · 273 comments · 1 d · RohanAdwankar",
   "size": "xs",
   "color": 6
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 184,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "Any hits? Do models even know their own weights to be able to do this? Probably yes, because they have been\npresumably trained on their own output and conversations about themselves.",
   "lines": 2
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 266,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "No, they would probably have to hack the internal system of the company running them. It would not be a\nparticularly wide ranging hack. There is a strong likelihood of the weights being on the actual machine that is\nrunning the model.",
   "lines": 3
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 383,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "It is something that I have wondered about with models like these. How many physical locations are needed to\nserve a model on that scale? Do they have a huge number of sites running inference?",
   "lines": 2
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 465,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "My suspicion is that the ability to provide inference to that many people is mutually exclusive to having a\nsecurity level sufficient to stop a state actor, which is why the question keeps coming back.",
   "lines": 2
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 547,
   "w": 1340,
   "text": "The weights are the easy part. Serving them at that scale is what needs a building, and buildings are the thing\nyou cannot hide.",
   "lines": 2
  },
  {
